19:Tf00,## The lines carry the meaning

Two boxes joined by a plain line tell you almost nothing. The **kind** of line, and **which end** its decoration touches, is where the design actually lives. Read a class diagram and the arrows tell you, at a glance, *how coupled* the system is and *what breaks when something changes*.

Think of relationships as a spectrum of **commitment**, from a one-night handshake to a marriage:

| Relationship | Symbol | Plain English | "Has-a"? | Lifecycle |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Dependency | ⇢ dashed | "uses for a moment" | no | unrelated |
| Association | → | "knows / refers to" | a reference | independent |
| Aggregation | ◇ hollow ◆ | "has-a, shared" | yes | part outlives whole |
| Composition | ◆ filled | "owns" | yes | part dies with whole |
| Inheritance | ▷ triangle | "is-a" |, | a *type*, not ownership |

The one question that separates the "has-a" trio: **if I destroy the whole, what happens to the part?**

## ⇢ Dependency: "uses transiently"

The weakest link. A only **mentions** B, as a method **parameter**, a local variable, or a return type. Nothing is stored.

*Analogy:* you use a **taxi** to get to the airport. You don't own it; once you arrive, the relationship is over.

```
class Order {
    Money total(TaxService tax) {   // tax is just borrowed for the call
        return subtotal + tax.on(subtotal);
    }
}
```

*Spot it in code:* B appears only inside a method signature — never as a field.

## → Association: "holds a reference"

A keeps a **long-lived reference** to B as a **field**, but neither created nor owns the other. They're peers.

*Analogy:* a **driver and a car** — the driver has a car they drive for years, but the car was bought elsewhere and could be sold without the driver disappearing.

```
class Driver { Car car; }   // a stored, long-lived link
```

## ◇ Aggregation vs ◆ Composition, the one everyone confuses

Both are **"has-a"**. Both are drawn with a diamond on the **whole** (owner) end. The *only* difference is **who controls the part's lifecycle**, and that's exactly what interviewers probe.

- **◇ Aggregation (hollow diamond), shared, the part outlives the whole.**
  *Analogy:* a **team and its players**. Disband the team and the players keep playing — they can join another team. The team didn't *create* them and doesn't *destroy* them.
  ```
  class Team { List<Player> roster; }   // players passed in from outside
  ```

- **◆ Composition (filled diamond), owned, the part dies with the whole.**
  *Analogy:* a **house and its rooms**. The house *creates* its rooms, and when you demolish the house the rooms are gone. A room can't belong to two houses.
  ```
  class House { House(){ rooms = new Room[n]; } }   // house makes & owns them
  ```

The litmus test: **"can the part exist on its own, or be shared?"** Yes → aggregation. No, it's created-and-destroyed by the owner → composition.

## ▷ Inheritance, a different axis

`SavingsAccount` **is-a** `Account`. This is about **type and substitutability**, not ownership, so it isn't on the strength scale at all. (It's covered in the Inheritance lesson.) The hollow triangle points at the parent.

## The rule of thumb

> **Prefer the weakest relationship that does the job:** dependency < association < aggregation < composition. Weaker binding = looser coupling = a design you can change without a domino effect. Reach for composition only when one object genuinely *owns* another's entire life.

**Common mistakes**
- Drawing **composition** when the part is actually shared or injected → it's aggregation.
- Storing a class as a field "just in case" when a method parameter (dependency) would do → needless coupling.
- Treating inheritance as a *has-a* shortcut for reuse → prefer composition (see "Composition over Inheritance").c:["$","div",null,{"className":"flex min-h-screen","children":[["$","$L17",null,{"activeModule":"lld-foundations","activeLesson":"uml-relationships"}],["$","$L18","uml-relationships",{"lesson":{"id":"uml-relationships","patternId":"lld-foundations","title":"UML Relationships","kind":"concept","difficulty":"Core","statement":"The arrows ARE the design. Dependency, association, aggregation, composition and inheritance say exactly how tightly two classes are bound, and who owns whose lifecycle.","companies":["Amazon","Google","Atlassian","Microsoft"],"theory":"$19","approaches":[{"label":"The strength spectrum","code":["// ⇢ depend, uses a parameter, stores nothing","class Order  { total(TaxService t){ ... } }","","// →  assoc, holds a long-lived reference","class Driver { Car car; }","","// ◇ aggregate, has-a, shared, part outlives whole","class Team   { List<Player> roster; }","","// ◆ compose, owns, parts die with the whole","class House  { House(){ rooms = new Room[n]; } }"],"steps":[{"view":"class","codeLine":2,"narration":"DEPENDENCY (⇢, the weakest). Order just USES TaxService, it’s a method parameter, nothing is stored on Order. When the call ends, the link is gone. Like taking a taxi: you use it, you don’t own it.","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"order","name":"Order","methods":["+ total()"],"x":0,"y":0.3},{"id":"tax","name":"TaxService","x":1,"y":0.3},{"id":"driver","name":"Driver","x":0,"y":1.6},{"id":"car","name":"Car","x":1,"y":1.6},{"id":"team","name":"Team","x":1.95,"y":0.3},{"id":"player","name":"Player","x":2.7,"y":0.3},{"id":"house","name":"House","x":1.95,"y":1.6},{"id":"room","name":"Room","x":2.7,"y":1.6}],"relations":[{"id":"dep","from":"order","to":"tax","kind":"depend","label":"uses"},{"id":"asc","from":"driver","to":"car","kind":"assoc","label":"drives"},{"id":"agg","from":"team","to":"player","kind":"aggregate","label":"has","toMult":"*"},{"id":"cmp","from":"house","to":"room","kind":"compose","label":"owns","toMult":"1..*"}],"shownClassIds":["order","tax"],"shownRelIds":["dep"],"activeIds":["order","tax"]},"vars":[["⇢ depend","uses transiently (a param)"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":5,"narration":"ASSOCIATION (→). A Driver keeps a long-lived REFERENCE to a Car as a field. Neither owns the other, both were made elsewhere and live independently. A stored link between peers.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["order","tax","driver","car"],"shownRelIds":["dep","asc"],"activeIds":["driver","car"]},"vars":[["→ assoc","holds a long-lived reference"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":8,"narration":"AGGREGATION (◇ hollow diamond). A Team HAS players, but the players exist without the team, disband it and every Player lives on, maybe joining another. The part is SHARED and outlives the whole. The diamond sits on the whole.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["order","tax","driver","car","team","player"],"shownRelIds":["dep","asc","agg"],"activeIds":["team","player"]},"vars":[["◇ aggregate","has-a, part outlives whole"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":11,"narration":"COMPOSITION (◆ filled diamond), the strongest. A House OWNS its Rooms: it creates them, and they belong to no one else. Demolish the House and the Rooms go too, their lifecycle is bound to the owner.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["order","tax","driver","car","team","player","house","room"],"shownRelIds":["dep","asc","agg","cmp"],"activeIds":["house","room"]},"vars":[["◆ compose","owns, lifecycle-bound"]]},{"view":"class","narration":"All four on one legend, weakest to strongest. The decoration is small but the meaning is huge: it tells the next reader exactly how coupled these classes are.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:0:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["order","tax","driver","car","team","player","house","room"],"shownRelIds":["dep","asc","agg","cmp"],"activeIds":[]},"vars":[["strength","depend < assoc < aggregate < compose"],["rule","pick the weakest that works"]]}]},{"label":"What dies with the whole?","code":["house.demolish();   // ◆ composition → its Rooms are destroyed too","team.disband();     // ◇ aggregation → its Players live on"],"steps":[{"view":"class","narration":"Aggregation and composition look almost identical, both are “has-a”, both draw a diamond on the whole. So how do you choose? Ask the only question that matters: destroy the whole, what happens to the part?","classDiagram":{"classes":[{"id":"house","name":"House","methods":["+ demolish()"],"x":0.2,"y":0.4},{"id":"room","name":"Room","x":1.5,"y":0.4},{"id":"team","name":"Team","methods":["+ disband()"],"x":0.2,"y":1.7},{"id":"player","name":"Player","x":1.5,"y":1.7}],"relations":[{"id":"c","from":"house","to":"room","kind":"compose","label":"owns","toMult":"*"},{"id":"a","from":"team","to":"player","kind":"aggregate","label":"has","toMult":"*"}],"shownClassIds":["house","room","team","player"],"shownRelIds":["c","a"],"activeIds":[]},"vars":[["the question","kill the whole → part?"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":1,"narration":"Call house.demolish(). COMPOSITION (◆): the House created and owns its Rooms, so the Rooms are destroyed with it. Watch them vanish, a Room can’t outlive its House.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["team","player"],"shownRelIds":["c","a"],"activeIds":[]},"vars":[["◆ composition","parts DIE with the whole"]]},{"view":"class","codeLine":2,"narration":"Now call team.disband(). AGGREGATION (◇): the Team merely grouped its Players, it never owned them. The Team is gone, but every Player LIVES ON, free to join another team.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["player"],"shownRelIds":["c","a"],"activeIds":["player"]},"vars":[["◇ aggregation","parts SURVIVE the whole"]]},{"view":"class","narration":"That single behaviour IS the whole distinction. Same diamond, opposite lifecycle: composition’s parts die with the owner; aggregation’s parts are shared and survive. Pick the filled diamond only when the owner truly controls the part’s entire life.","classDiagram":{"classes":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:classes","relations":"$c:props:children:1:props:lesson:approaches:1:steps:0:classDiagram:relations","shownClassIds":["player"],"shownRelIds":["c","a"],"activeIds":[]},"vars":[["◆ filled","owned → dies"],["◇ hollow","shared → survives"]]}]}]}}]]}]
